<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Hardened-runtime entitlements for stack-nudge.app.

        We bundle a Python interpreter (python-build-standalone) plus
        stackvox's transitive deps (Kokoro + ONNX runtime) inside the
        .app at Contents/Resources/venv/. Hardened runtime is required
        for notarization, but its defaults block exactly the patterns an
        embedded interpreter relies on. These three opt-ins keep us
        notarization-eligible while letting the bundled Python work.

        allow-jit + allow-unsigned-executable-memory:
          ONNX runtime and PyTorch (when present as a transitive dep)
          JIT-compile inference kernels at runtime, writing executable
          pages to memory. Hardened runtime blocks this by default.

        disable-library-validation:
          The bundled venv contains .so / .dylib extensions whose
          identity differs from the .app's signing identity. Library
          validation would reject them; this opt-out is standard for
          apps shipping their own Python.

        These entitlements weaken the sandbox protections we'd otherwise
        inherit. The trade-off is intentional and matches the precedent
        set by other Python-bundled Mac apps (Beam, Reflex's app bundles,
        etc.). Notarization accepts the combination.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
